decisions:
  - id: swiftterm-validated
    decision: SwiftTerm is production-viable for Conductor embedded terminals
    rationale: Sprint 0 spike proved Claude Code renders correctly. Jinx's concerns about OSC 133 and performance were not observed. All 4 workspace sprints shipped.
  - id: rune-8th-core-agent
    decision: Rune (compliance) added as 8th core agent for symbol planning and coverage validation
    rationale: 15+ files were created this session without proper aspects, flows, or signals. Documentor handles post-implementation; Rune handles pre-implementation symbol planning and mid-implementation compliance.
  - id: university-restructure-plan
    decision: '8-phase university restructuring: add PARA 001, fix agent disconnect, add enforcement/quick-check/Rune content, replace memorization quizzes'
    rationale: Audit found courses teach WHAT (60% in 101) more than HOW. No day-1 course exists. Enforcement config, quick-check mode, Rune, and Conductor workspace are not covered in core 101-501 path.
  - id: pan-evolution-consensus
    decision: PAN sits ON TOP of agent frameworks. A/B experiment is priority zero. Cut ReAct/Tool Registry/Agent Network, replace with registry MVP + framework bridge.
    rationale: Cross-team Symphony debate. Original 6-phase plan spent 80% on runtime (AgentScope territory) and 0% on distribution. Revised plan proves the thesis first, builds the marketplace second.
  - id: conductor-workspace-mode
    decision: Conductor is now a full workspace application with embedded terminals
    rationale: '4 sprints: SwiftTerm embedding, session manager + multi-pane, Symphony auto-link, keyboard shortcuts + workspace mode. Overlay mode preserved as alternative.'
  - id: minimal-enforcement-default
    decision: Default enforcement changed from balanced to minimal for new projects
    rationale: New users were getting blocked by stop hooks before understanding .purpose files. Minimal warns but never blocks. Users upgrade to balanced/strict when ready.
  - id: shift-non-interactive
    decision: paradigm shift runs non-interactively by default, auto-detects model tiers
    rationale: Interactive model selection prompt blocked the one-command setup promise. Now auto-defaults based on environment detection.
learnings:
  - SwiftTerm handles Claude Code's Ink TUI flawlessly — validated empirically via spike, not theoretically
  - NSEvent.addLocalMonitorForEvents does NOT intercept keys consumed by performKeyEquivalent — use window-level performKeyEquivalent override for shortcuts that need to fire before SwiftTerm
  - SwiftTerm's LocalProcessTerminalView eats mouse events — use NSEvent local monitor with bounds check for focus detection, and @ObservedObject on child views for immediate SwiftUI redraw
  - Always consult the team before implementing — even 'simple' changes have architectural implications. The user corrected this twice.
  - University courses should teach HOW before WHAT — put hands-on experience in the first 10 minutes, theory after
